  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-{90}^{∘})=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{90}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-{90}^{∘})=\\operatorname{csc}⁡({270}^{∘}).$\n\nThe angle ${270}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253609/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${270}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({270}^{∘})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡({270}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({270}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-{90}^{∘})=-1.$",
